<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. London asked for a same-day visit. Yesterday evening, walking the pasture at dusk, he stepped into a burrow and turned his right foot inward; he felt a sharp pain on the outside of the ankle and limped back to the house. The ankle swelled overnight. He took two acetaminophen tablets and, he says, two large glasses of whisky for the pain: the first alcohol since he stopped 27 days ago. He did not take naproxen, though there was still a bottle in the house; he has thrown it out this morning. He walked in from the car with a limp. He has had no thoughts of harming himself and the firearms are still locked away. He takes naltrexone every morning and asks whether he can have something stronger for the pain. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Yesterday evening, an inversion injury</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Outer side of the right ankle</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>About 16 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Aching and throbbing; sharp on walking</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>6 of 10 when walking</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Constant, worse on standing</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Chronic kidney disease; on naltrexone; one evening of drinking last night</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Acetaminophen and rest help; walking hurts</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Swelling and bruising; no numbness; no other injury</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

