    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Tiredness, ankle swelling and high blood pressure (158/98 mmHg) with a raised creatinine (1.98 mg/dL, eGFR 43) and albumin in the urine (240 mg/g), found at intake; a creatinine of 1.7 mg/dL at an insurance examination about fourteen months ago, by his account; chronicity and cause under evaluation with a nephrology consultation</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R94.4</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to the documented kidney damage found in 1913; superseded when the consultation staged chronic kidney disease.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Chronic kidney disease, stage G3b A2 (eGFR 42 to 43, urine albumin 240 mg/g), most likely from hypertension with a contribution from two years of daily naproxen; kidneys of normal size, without stones or obstruction; urine sediment bland; synthetic modern diagnosis linked to his documented kidney disease</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>N18.32</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the consultation, anchored to his documented kidney damage and death from uraemia; the cause in the modern episode is synthetic and is not a claim about his real kidneys.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Hypertensive chronic kidney disease, untreated until intake</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I12.9</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Alcohol use disorder, severe (seven DSM-5-TR criteria): a bottle of whisky or more most days for years, a morning drink to steady his hands, failed attempts to cut down, and drinking despite knowing it harms his health; AUDIT 27; PEth 410 ng/mL</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F10.20</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis anchored to his own published account of heavy drinking; the criteria, scores and amounts are synthetic and are not a claim about his real drinking.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Alcohol-associated fatty liver without advanced fibrosis (liver stiffness 7.2 kPa; steatosis on ultrasound); FIB-4 2.28 at intake</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>K70.0</td><td>S — Synthetic finding of the ultrasound and elastography at the consultation; not a claim about his real liver.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Daily self-directed naproxen for knee and back pain for about two years, stopped at intake; acetaminophen in its place</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z79.1</td><td>S — Synthetic medication history; the joint pain is anchored to the documented rheumatism of 1914, which is not given a diagnosis.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-7">
    <td>Cigarette smoking, about ten a day; not ready to stop while he stops drinking</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F17.210</td><td>S — Synthetic tobacco use; a physician-author&apos;s suggestion of heavy tobacco use is recorded only as a retrospective claim.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-8">
    <td>Right ankle sprain (lateral ligaments, moderate), from stepping into a burrow while walking the pasture; Ottawa ankle rules negative; healed in about four weeks</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>S93.401A</td><td>S — Synthetic acute injury; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

